  <section|Mathematical content written><label|mathematical-content-written>

  <\enumerate>
    <item>The atom estimate uses the reviewed row-slot complex extension on a
    common link-variable ball. The degree-r product is bounded by the atom
    budget at width <verbatim|sigma/2>; Cauchy's derivative cost is
    <verbatim|circleTax(R,t+1)/(t+1)!>; the momentum strip is chosen by
    <verbatim|4(t+1)^2 eta=sigma/2>. This makes the term cost exactly a fixed
    constant times the full <verbatim|budget(sigma)>. Absolute convergence
    permits differentiation and finite anchor summation. The orientation
    average charges the product of reflected-character fees.

    <item>The Ward contraction is explicitly the sum over spatial axes with
    the external color word fixed, matching <verbatim|crux>. The
    all-singleton term is the exact top-partition term <verbatim|elem_top>;
    all other partitions use pictures of order <verbatim|b-1> for derivatives
    of order <verbatim|b=2,...,t>. Density of zero-total momentum meshes
    extends the identity to real momenta. The entry-ray step now follows the
    <verbatim|ring_ray>/<verbatim|rayDark>/<verbatim|rayBlack> argument:
    first isolate the constant with one coordinate test; then use the
    identity at <verbatim|q+w> minus at <verbatim|q> to kill derivatives
    transverse to a leg; two legs kill the full differential.

    <item>The cubic and quartic streams give order-2 and order-3 pictures
    through four atom estimates and the Ward result. Their zero lower
    coefficient supplies the lower input for the order-2 Ward step. The
    curvature stream contributes the order-1 zero picture, then
    <verbatim|veneer> combines the three records with ray orders
    <verbatim|3,2,1> for <verbatim|cureCore>.

    <item>The Wilson-subtracted estimate assembles the metric-face picture,
    row pictures of orders 2 and 3, and Wilson pictures. The order-1
    difference has ray order 3: the attached Hessian identity and floor/sworn
    inputs give <verbatim|B(0)=I>, and the Fourier expression is
    <verbatim|curl*(B(p)-I)curl>, with each curl <verbatim|O(\|p\|)> and
    <verbatim|B(p)-I=O(\|p\|)>. <verbatim|whites_on_K> supplies the exact
    input-series identification and lower lawful sums; <verbatim|pure_grey>
    upgrades the higher records. The stated price is the sum of the
    metric/leader charges, row and Wilson prices, and the cubic/quartic
    budget.

    <item>The local response construction now uses the exact source route
    rather than generic polarization. At level <verbatim|n=0,1,2>, the mixed
    derivative has <verbatim|n> slots of one split-field species and two of
    the other. The ordered partition chain rule has zero one-block term
    (<verbatim|DU(0)=0>); each block term uses an order <verbatim|b-1> input
    picture, and every mixed picture has ray order <verbatim|2-n>. Its finite
    coefficient bill is the sum over partitions and basis assignments of
    <verbatim|b!> times products of absolute split-field derivative
    coefficients. The width loss is <verbatim|calls(n+2,sigma)=sigma/(n+3)^2>.

    <item>The finite division peels exactly <verbatim|2-n> one-link factors.
    Level 0 routes one factor to each of its two legs using
    <verbatim|q(p,a)=-mode(p,step a)q(-p,a)>; level 1 uses one factor; level
    2 uses none. Contour shifting bounds the weighted Laurent coefficient sum
    at a smaller width. The array addresses are the finite port keys and
    external color words; the factorization and Fourier character
    orthogonality give the exact finite-period response identity. The output
    therefore has the mathematical content of <verbatim|Fruit> (uniform
    <verbatim|Dai> width) and <verbatim|Syrupy> (the <verbatim|Bitter>
    response identity), assembled from the three levelwise
    <verbatim|loanChar> constructions and their common minimum width / summed
    price.
  </enumerate>

